WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.360
Scenes can be cut out of movies for any number
of reasons.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.380
Sometimes they grind the plot to a halt, while
other times they clash with the tone of the

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:08.260
rest of the movie.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.000
Often, they’re just totally unnecessary.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.620
These deleted scenes, however, are some of
the greatest ever filmed which makes it even

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.360
more of a shame that they ended up on the
cutting room floor.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.360
Arguably the most important and horrifying
scene in Andy Muschietti's [MUH-shee-EH-tee]

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:27.900
adaptation of It is the debut of Pennywise,
Stephen King's unforgettable "Eater of Worlds."

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.260
Spawning a thousand memes and a thousand more
nightmares, the moment when Pennywise lures

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:38.829
Georgie Denbrough into the sewers below Derry
is as terrifying as it is instantly memorable,

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:41.600
and it's hard to imagine the situation could
have played out differently.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.080
But it turns out that Muschietti actually
directed a surprise alternate opening for

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.360
It which ends in a very different way.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.900
“See you later, bye!”

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.280
While this was obviously filmed as a gag,
Muschietti's alternate opening does provoke

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.280
some interesting thoughts about the story.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.860
If Georgie had survived Pennywise's attack,
it's entirely possible that the evil demon’s

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.900
reign over the town of Derry would have continued
unchallenged.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.600
After all, the murder of Georgie was the beginning
of the end of of his demise even if he didn’t

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:17.640
know it at the time.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.280
When we first meet Steve Rogers in The Avengers,
he’s taking out his frustration and despair

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.500
on a punching bag, as memories of his life
during World War II flash through his mind.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.340
But there’s also an entire deleted sequence
which demonstrates just how depressed Cap

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.360
has really become.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:37.780
The scene opens with Steve, alone in his apartment,
watching World War II propaganda films.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.100
After poring through the government files
of his long-gone friends, he briefly considers

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.940
calling his old flame Peggy Carter but never
picks up the phone.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.600
Steve then wanders through a city filled with
cell phones and fancy cars, completely lost

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.500
in the 21st century, before winding up at
a cafe.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.580
“Ask for her number, you moron.”

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.880
He then takes a lonely subway ride to the
boxing gym where he meet him in the actual

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:04.380
film.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.600
It's a shame this sequence was cut from the
movie, since it shows the full extent of Steve's

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:13.440
isolation and makes it a more satisfying victory
when he finally finds his place in the world.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:18.640
Marvel movie dads are often rotten, and Thanos
has got to be the worst of the bunch.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.560
Even his favorite daughter Gamora has suffered
a fair bit of abuse at his hands but while

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.540
their relationship is incredibly twisted,
the two still feel some affection for each

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.870
other, and Infinity War did a good job of
exploring their complex dynamic in a short

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:32.640
amount of time.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.960
However, there's one deleted scene that digs
even deeper into their relationship.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:41.440
It opens in Thanos' throne room, with the
Mad Titan showing his imprisoned daughter

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.870
a scene from their past, back when she was
his most trusted assassin.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.000
“By the look of it, you seemed content enough
at the time.”

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.180
“I was a prisoner.”

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.760
But Gamora explains that that her self-loathing
only started to disappear when she joined

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.520
up with the Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.220
Even a guilt trip from Thanos isn’t enough
to get her back onside.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.560
“You had a family here, little one - you
just didn’t care what happened to it.”

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.740
While the scene isn't exactly necessary to
the story, it does give some color to one

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.020
of the movie’s most fascinating relationships.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.660
A deleted scene doesn't need to be long to
be great.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.100
Take this moment from Spider-Man 2, for example.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.500
The scene comes after Peter Parker has given
up on his crime-fighting career, and is finally

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.440
ready to live a normal life having thrown
his suit into a garbage can.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.040
Eventually, the suit makes its way to J. Jonah
Jameson.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.680
The Daily Bugle’s cantankerous editor is
thrilled that Spider-Man has called it quits,

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.260
but in this deleted scene we learn that maybe
there’s a reason why Jameson dislikes Spidey

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:43.900
so much.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.520
“Pew pew pew.”

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:53.280
Even though this scene is incredibly brief,
it's a hysterical moment that suggests Jameson

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.200
might be a bigger Spider-Man fan than he cares
to admit.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.040
The weightlifting sequence is one of the best
moments from M. Night Shyamalan's Unbreakable.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.380
“How much is it, Dad?”

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.200
“About 350 pounds.”

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.240
It's a triumphant moment for the self-doubting
David, but in one deleted scene he gets an

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:16.020
extra bit of reassurance that he's really
something special.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:20.440
After impressing his kid, David goes to visit
the weight room at the football stadium where

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.860
he works and successfully lifts nearly 500
pounds.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.420
It's a nice comic sting to close the scene,
although, as Shyamalan himself has pointed

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.540
out, it was just a bit "redundant" coming
straight after the basement sequence.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.560
But that doesn’t mean it’s not a lot of
fun.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.020
Hannibal Lecter is a stone cold psychopath.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.740
He’s a genuine monster who feeds on others,
both emotionally and literally — but while

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.150
serial killers aren't usually big on feelings,
there's a deleted scene from The Silence of

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.440
the Lambs that shows Hannibal is actually
capable of empathy.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:59.380
Locked away in his underworld prison, Hannibal
launches into a monologue about the psychology

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.400
behind his fellow killer, Buffalo Bill.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.280
“Our Billy wasn’t born a killer, Clarice.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.580
Oh no, he was made one through years of systematic
abuse.”

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.870
Tellingly, Hannibal isn’t speaking in his
usual condescending tone — you can actually

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.520
hear the sympathy in his voice.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:23.260
“Billy’s houses will be very small, with
tiny windows; no flowers no pets no toys,

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.220
no sun.”

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.000
He ends his impassioned speech with a line
that is both chilling and strangely sad.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.080
"Our Billy wants to be reborn, Clarice.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.820
And he will be reborn.”

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.360
Only segments of Hannibal's speech are heard
in the movie’s theatrical cut, but had the

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:45.070
original scene remained intact, it might have
offered audiences a deeper look into Bill’s

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:48.500
twisted mind and a better understanding of
Hannibal Lecter himself.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.480
Captain Quint has got to be one of the greatest
movie characters of all time.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:58.040
Played by the inimitable Robert Shaw, Quint
is a salty sea dog who loves beer, hates sharks,

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.640
and has fun antagonizing "wealthy college
boys."

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.240
It soon becomes clear that there's a lot more
going on beneath Quint’s strange exterior,

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:09.360
but early in the film he’s played as a cackling
coot with a bizarre sense of humor.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.760
One deleted scene from Jaws demonstrates just
how odd Quint can be.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.290
Requiring some fishing line that’s strong
enough to catch sharks, he drops by the local

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.180
music store to buy some piano wire.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:24.680
There, he finds a young boy practicing the
clarinet and starts to sing along.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:33.700
“Bap bap bap bap bap bap bap bap.”

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Granted, the scene doesn't really contribute
much to the film and would've done some damage

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:42.389
to the story’s pacing, but it’s a hilarious
performance from Shaw and a welcome chance

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:44.200
to spend some more time with Quint.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:49.000
One of the defining character traits of Lethal
Weapon’s Martin Riggs is his unflinching

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.060
willingness to put himself in harm’s way.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:55.830
This is a guy who’s always hoping that somebody
will end his lonely life and his desire for

00:06:55.830 --> 00:07:00.940
death is made all the clearer in this deleted
scene, where Riggs squares off against a schoolyard

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:01.440
sniper.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:06.100
Riggs gets a call that there's a gunman firing
at a school, and the SWAT team isn't going

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.400
to show up anytime soon.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.220
Naturally, he makes his move.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:15.660
“Get down get down you're in the line of fire”

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:21.580
When he finally reaches the sniper, he doesn’t
hold back.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:34.780
Yeah, it's not entirely necessary to the plot,
but don’t pretend it’s not badass.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:39.840
Few deleted scenes have captured the hearts
of movie lovers quite like the "Jitterbug"

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.000
musical number that was cut from The Wizard
of Oz.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:46.960
Clocking in at six whole minutes, the dance
sequence took five weeks to rehearse and perform,

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.700
costing MGM a total of $80,000.

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:54.520
Reports suggest that the scene was ultimately
cut to reduce the film's running time, but

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:58.650
it's also possible that the upbeat nature
of the song simply didn't fit with the story’s

00:07:58.650 --> 00:07:59.620
darker elements.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:03.200
Written by composer Harold Arlen and lyricist
E.Y.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:07.330
Harburg, the scene was based around a pink
and blue insect that caused its victims to

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.120
dance uncontrollably once bitten.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:13.400
Although the Jitterbug wasn't included in
the movie’s theatrical cut, hints towards

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:14.780
its existence still remain.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.280
“I’ve sent a little insect to take the
fight out of them.”

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:22.360
It just goes to show some things can’t be
disposed of so easily.

